\section{Pushdown}{

\code{filter} and \code{select} are pushed down into scan planning, which is the whole
performance argument for Iceberg over reading raw Parquet: manifests carry
per-file statistics, so entire files and row groups are skipped before any
bytes are read. Inspect the effect with \code{\link[=icebergr_scan_plan]{icebergr_scan_plan()}}.

\code{limit} is \strong{not} pushed down: \code{iceberg-rust} has no row limit in its scan
API, so the same files are planned and rows are counted as batches arrive.
It bounds how much is decoded and converted, not how much is planned.

Filters may use \code{==}, \code{!=}, \code{<}, \code{<=}, \code{>}, \code{>=}, \code{&}, \code{|}, \code{!}, \code{\%in\%},
\code{is.na()}, \code{is.nan()} and \code{startsWith()}. A bare name is read as a column when
the table has a column of that name, and otherwise evaluated in the calling
environment, so \code{filter = year == target} works with a local \code{target}.
Anything more elaborate should be applied in R after collecting.

\code{startsWith()} is pushed down only against a \code{string} column, since Iceberg
defines a prefix comparison for no other type.

A filter on a \code{decimal} column is pushed down, but with \code{iceberg-rust}'s
row-level selection turned off for that scan: in 0.10.0 that stage drops every
row of an ordering comparison against a decimal, so \code{price > 2.25} returned
nothing at all. File and row-group pruning still apply, so such a scan is a
little less selective and still correct.
